A||N||9||SloPonti||NEW MOTOR STARTED-NOW A FEW QUESTIONS!!!||||6||
Z||000000||SloPonti||01-21-2000||09:21 AM||||as you all know i got my new four cylinder back into the car last weekend-you know the purple thing! anyways this week at night i have hooked everything back up and last night i tried to start it-which it did do! sounds great with the header and dual exhaust! i had the motor running for about 15-20 minutes to let the cam break in! after that time i had to turn it off because it was really really hot but the temp gauge was only about 150 but it was so hot that the paint on the  head starting to burn off! the antifreeze did not boil but it did get hot! also the antifreeze does not smell burnt! do you guys think that this is just air locks in the coolent tubes! if i sqeeze the tubes you can here coolent noises kinda-sounds kinda like bubles or air pockets in there! how do i fix this!<BR>thanks<BR>Rob<BR>also why would my temp gauge only read about 150 when i now it was hotter than that! i did check to make sure that all sensors were hooked up right! would an air pocket give this a false reading? thanks||||reg||6
Z||000001||Phil||01-21-2000||10:37 AM||||Were the rad. hoses hot? If not you have air pockets, I'm not sure on the bleeding procedure for a 4cyl.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phaeton||01-21-2000||11:04 AM||||Newly rebuilt motors have a lot of internal friction until all the pieces wear together, hot runningduring initial breakin is a little normal, but not to cook paint.  Antifreeze does not conduct heat near as well as straight water with maybe a little water soluble oil in it, then put the antifreeze back in afer breakin.  How tight were the pistons and what grit finish hone did they get? Just me being curious for my own reference.||||reg||1
Z||000003||SloPonti||01-21-2000||01:17 PM||||the rad hoses were not very hot! also it did not burn the paint-just discolored it a little around the back of the head where the new header joins the head! i did use a mix of water and antifreeze! what do you guys suggest i do!<BR>thanks<BR>Rob||||reg||11
Z||000004||ChadMan||01-21-2000||01:25 PM||||I was able to get rid of all the air bubbles in my cooling system by jacking up the rear end, removing the thermostat cover and thermostat, and running the car for a few minutes while watching the coolant level.  Each time a bubble broke free, the coolant level would drop, and I'd add more at the thermostat housing.  After a couple of minutes, the coolant level stopped dropping, and actually began to rise as the engine warmed up.  At this point I shut the car off, replaced the thermostat and cover, and topped off the coolant reservoir tank.  No more bubbles!<P>If you've already done all this, just ignore me.||||reg||1
Z||000005||William Federle||01-21-2000||01:32 PM||||After spending all of the time and money putting that good looking engine in your car I think that if you're worried about the temp. that you should get one of those Auto Xray code scanners that theogre is always talking about and find out exactly what it is. I know my gauge is reading about half of what the temp really is. I think that the optimum mix for antifreeze is 50/50 (for ethylene glcol). ||||reg||1
Z||000006||DRH||01-21-2000||02:44 PM||||SloPonti,<P>See the link below for a method of filling coolant. From my experinence I think the key things are; fill from engine with radiator cap off, leave t-stat out run for short periods, and repeat several times.<P>Some discoloring of the paint near the manifold (header in your case) is normal.<P>Fiero temp gages are not known for accuracy. You might want to take steps to see if 150 is correct (as suggested above). As long as you are in doubt, don't run it too long at a time. You don't want to ruin a new engine  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/cooling-system.html#bleed" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/cooling-system.html#bleed</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||01-21-2000||03:41 PM||||Sometimes it's very hard to completely burp out all the air out of the coolant lines. This is a big pain in the butt with Fieros. Yes, if the temp sender is in an air pocket, it will not read correctly.||||reg||1
Z||000008||penetrator||01-21-2000||03:56 PM||||Also check the thermostat!!!<P>I have changed 2 thermostats what seamed to be sealed with that "juice" stuff that comes from the coolant water, after, when all the previos owners never flushed the coolant system.<P>Then i flushed the coolant system and cleand the thermostats and they all worked fine. <P>Check that if you haven´t.  =)<P>Thanks<BR>Magnus||||reg||1
Z||000009||SloPonti||01-21-2000||05:40 PM||||thanks guys! i think i am going to buy a aftermarket temp gauge and hook it up in the motor compartment while i do my next few startups so that way i know exactly what my temp is! i think i will also do the short startup procedures! i think that air bubbles just have to work themselves out! all i can do is watch and try to help them out! thanks for all the advice! i can't wait to drive my fiero!!!!!!!<BR>Rob||||reg||6
